<!--
    Reads of an expression C evaluates wider or with another sign than the
    machine operation that computes it.
        widen: movzx eax,word [rsi]; xor eax,0x80; add eax,-0x80
               mov [rdi],rax; ret
        shift: movzx eax,word [rdi]; xor eax,0x80; add eax,-0x80
               shr eax,3; ret
    Both 32-bit results are typed unsigned, but C promotes the loaded
    unsigned short to int, so the printed `(*a1 ^ 0x80) - 0x80` is an int:
    widening it to 64 bits sign-extends where the 32-bit operation
    zero-extends, and `>> 3` shifts arithmetically where shr shifts in
    zeros.  For a halfword of 0x80 the binary stores 0x00000000ffffff80 and
    returns 0x1ffffff0; the old C stored 0xffffffffffffff80 and returned
    0xfffffff0.  Such a read now makes the expression unsigned: here the
    constant of its top operation prints 0x80U (with no such constant the
    expression is cast to unsigned int).
        sel (gcc -O0, switch ((unsigned char)(c + 1)) with cases 0x80..0x84):
               movzx eax,byte [rbp-4]; add eax,1; movzx eax,al
               add eax,-0x80; cmp eax,4; ja default; jmp [rax*8+0x402000]
    The selector is a byte sum, but C adds the promoted char and 1 as ints:
    for c = 0x7f the old `switch(a0 + '\x01')` switched on 128 and took the
    default where the binary takes case 0x80 (printed -0x80).  A switch on
    an expression narrower than int whose promotion is not known to extend
    like its type now casts the selector to that type.

    Stage: P9 emission (ActionSetCasts input casts; the switch selector).
    A strict fix with no option: #1, #3 and #5 are the corrected reads, #2,
    #4 and #6 the old ones.
-->
